A||||9||007DOUG||The plastic car that WILL rust||||12||
Z||000000||007DOUG||08-14-2000||12:21 PM||||Makes me think of a picture that hangs in a dentist office. "This is what will happen if you don't brush 3 times a day."<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierowarehouse/fierorust.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fierowarehouse/fierorust.jpg</A> <P>Northern owners/buyers beware. I've seen MUCH worse than this. One so big I could have stuck my head through the hole.<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000001||HugGRRRFiero||08-14-2000||12:39 PM||||   Here's a funny story, one day I looked at a Fiero a guy had for sale.  He pulled back the carpet in that same corner to show me the trunk area was in good shape.  After I took one look at it, I had to tell him that what he was looking at was the plastic inner wheelhouse panel.  The guy seemed a little embarrassed after realizing the car wasn't in as good of shape he thought...  Guess he thought since they were made of plastic that everything was plastic.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">    <BR>    <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A>   <p>[This message has been edited by HugGRRRFiero (edited 08-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fformula88||08-14-2000||05:11 PM||||Ah yes.  The rusting of the trunk ends.  I have seen some good examples of it.  All Fiero owners should be aware of this however... my 88 Formula has never been run in a winter, but still has some rust at the ends of the trunk.  Must be that moisture gets up in there from the outside.  <P>Speaking of rust, I wish everyone could see the underside of my dads 87 Coupe.  After 13 Buffalo winters, there isn't much left that isn't rusted!||||reg||1
Z||000003||rockcrawl||08-14-2000||07:11 PM||||Wow,<BR> I can't believe that rusted-out trunk! I live in north-east PA and I have never seen any Fiero like that. I've owned at least ten Fieros and I've never had one with a hole in the trunk. In fact I've never had any problems with rust, except the battery trays. Have I just been lucky?<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000004||trigger||08-14-2000||09:41 PM||||How do you fix the rust?  I've got a little bit of surface rust on the right trunk corner(not to bad but I'd like to fix), and a little on the right front of the cradle.  Thanks.<P>Anthony Wright<P>------------------<BR>Honestly officer, I was just keeping up with the flow of traffic.  I know there aren't any cars in the area, that's how far behind I am.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Black88GT||08-14-2000||09:48 PM||||I live in MD, it gets pretty cold here during the winter months.  I have only seen it on 2m4s (84-86 coupes).  Maybe its some kind of defect (yet another) in the earlier Fiero's because it seems to be only in them.  (yeah I know there older but maybe its something else?) Recently I checked for it on an 88 coupe that had been sitting for a while and it was fine too.<P><BR>jeff<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||My7Fieros||08-14-2000||11:06 PM||||Ahhh, one of the reasons Im glad I live in the south! No salted roads here! Well, maybe one in a blue moon. Typically when we get our 1 or 2 snowfalls each winter (usually maybe 2 or 3 inches at best), the ENTIRE city shuts down!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Gotta love those rust free cars!||||reg||6
Z||000007||fierospeeder||08-14-2000||11:11 PM||||I also have one in my fiero, its on the lower side panel, and if i ever replace it, i would only need a piece of sheet metal tp weld in.<P>All my brake lines are rusted, and my cradle is rusted in a few areas.  So when working underneath, i have to watch out that i dont get any rust particles in my eye.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Carrolles||08-16-2000||05:44 PM||||Get a can of Rust Converter.  You can get it at WalMart.  It is a spray can of basically phosphoric acid.  It turns iron oxide to iron phosphate.  It is usually found in the auto section where bondo filler is.<P>Brush off loose rust and scale and spray this clear liquid on.  It will turn the rust black.  You can then paint over it.  Works great especially where it is difficult to get rust out of crevices.||||reg||1
Z||000009||LarryB||08-16-2000||08:58 PM||||There are a couple of different types, spray on or brush on, they all work pretty much the same.  The key is to just remove the loose stuff and not to sand/grind all the rust off, the converter needs to have rust remaining on the surface to work properly.  <P>You can also get Rustoleum paint with rust converted added, I got some I think at Home Depot.  It works too and and you don't have to wait the 24 hrs to paint after applying the converter.  <P>While you're at it you might as well pull the battery and check the battery tray as well, that's another common problem area.<BR>||||reg||6
